{:nav-title "Index"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Learn Datomic Datalog"]
  [:p "This interactive website will help you learn how to query a Datomic database using Datalog."]
  [:p "Datalog is a declarative, logic-based query language. Like SQL, you describe "
   [:em "what"] " you want rather than " [:em "how"] " to find it. Unlike SQL, Datalog works by "
   "matching " [:strong "patterns"] " against facts, making it a natural fit for Datomic's "
   "data model."]
  [:p "By the end of this guide you will be able to model your own data, filter and aggregate query results, "
   "and traverse relationships between entities. You will also parameterize queries, pull whole entities back "
   "as data, write reusable rules, and query the database as it looked at any point in its history. "
   "Every example runs against a real database of all "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :pokemon/name _]]] " original Pokémon."]

  [:h2 "Who this is for"]
  [:p "This guide is for anyone curious about querying with Datomic, whether or not you've used it before. "
   "No SQL experience is required, though the comparisons above will click faster if you have some. "
   "What matters more is being able to read a little Clojure, since every query on this site is Clojure data:"]
  [:ul
   [:li [:code "[...]"] " is a vector, an ordered list."]
   [:li [:code ":like-this"] " is a keyword, Clojure's version of a name or a column label."]
   [:li "A symbol starting with " [:code "?"] ", like " [:code "?name"] ", is a "
    [:strong "logic variable"] ": a placeholder the query fills in."]
   [:li "Text in " [:code "\"quotes\""] " is a string."]
   [:li [:code ","] " is whitespace to Clojure. Some examples use " [:code ",,,"]
    " to visually separate parts of a query."]]
  [:p "For a deeper look at Clojure's data syntax, see "
   [:a {:href "https://clojure.org/reference/reader"} "the official reader reference"] "."]
  [:p "No Pokémon expertise is required either. Stats, types, generations, and terms like "
   "\"super-effective\" or \"legendary\" are explained as they come up, using the data itself."]

  [:p "Click the \"Run\" button below to run your first Datomic Datalog query."]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?entity :pokemon/type "Grass"]
    [?entity :pokemon/name ?name]]]
  [:p "That was a query. We will look at the facts it matched first, "
   "then at how a query is put together, "
   "and last at how the data got into the database."]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Edit the query above to return Pokémon of other types like Fire or Electric."]]]}
